ation about  people  the refugee experience and not not just  that people are refugees but that being  a refugee is something that you may  experience but you are also a lot of  other stuff around it like you see with  emin  and his life as  as an academic as a cat lover as eunice  likes to say and  that that story comes out to all these  audience who right now in this world  are  that we hope that they will also embrace  all the refugees that are still coming  out of afghanistan syria ukraine and and  places that our experiences were like  that thank you  and just to  introduce the clip um  well first of all you know  it's called flea and it is about the  fiscal flight going from afghanistan to  denmark and it's it is a refugee story  but it's really a story about home and  belonging and connectivity it's about  amin who is  a refugee and also gay and who for all  of his life has had to  hide past himself  when he was kid in afghanistan he  couldn't be openly gay when he arrived  in denmark he couldn't be honest to his  past  so all this life he's had to  hide um so this film is about a man  looking for a place in the world where  he feels he can be who he is  with everything that entails and the  clip you're going to see now is from our  very first conversations when he started  to open this black box it was in my home  in copenhagen nine years ago  yeah  thank you  [Applause]  oh  it  mm  [Music]  [Music]  be  [Music]  um  [Music]  [Music]  to  foreign  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  please welcome two of the nominees for  summer of seoul or when the revolution  could not be televised robert fiveland  and david dinnerstein  [Applause]  thank you guys um  first i want to say just you know what  an honor it is  to be nominated with these films and uh  it's just been an incredible experience  um we also wanted to um  say that quest love couldn't be here  tonight  uh our third producer joseph patel uh  also couldn't be here but  part of the  um  [Music]  sort of joy of the experience of working  on this film  was seeing quest love emerge as an  important new voice in  the documentary film world and  you know we're happy to have been a part  of that  um  i started on this film 15 years ago  i  flew to bronxville new york  and  convinced hal tolchin who had shot the  original footage  that  he should let me make a documentary  and i convinced him we'd build the right  team  um and we did  um and  you know for 15 years i kept  reminding him or you know convincing him  you know we'll get this we'll get this  done and  there were you know many times or  several times over that uh period of  time that the film almost got made and  um  you know we just couldn't get it over  the finish line and i i tell people all  the time thank god it it didn't happen  at any of those other times because this  was the right team and the right way to  tell the story  and  it's the right time in the country for  the story i i think and  i think people needed the joy  of and the celebration of this movie  coming out of the pandemic  and  so from that perspective  you know i'm happy that that this is the  version of the movie that got made  um i'll let david tell you a little bit  about the the clip that we're going to  see  sure uh before we do that i just wanted  to sort of thank the academy it's really  such a privilege to be in this beautiful  space  to watch all of these clips on this  amazing screen with this great sound uh  it's such an honor and i also wanted to  acknowledge uh two of our eps that are  in the house tonight and they just  provided just unbelievable of support  and that's marie therese girgis and  janet gargey  so thank you guys stan please  acknowledge yourselves  i also  you know just wanted to acknowledge and  i'll do this for robert too i wanted to  acknowledge my wife  allison who you know has been  watching me and and my wife brittany  and they've been watching us you know  i've been o